===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|subtilty}} {{qualifier|archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|sotilte}}, from {{der|en|fro|sutilté}}, inherited from {{der|en|la|subtīlitās}}, from {{m|la|subtīlis||subtle}}. Equivalent to {{suffix|en|subtle|ty}}. {{doublet|en|subtility}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsʌt(ə)lti/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-subtlety.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-subtlety.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The quality of being [[subtle]].
## (''of things'') The quality of being scarcely [[noticeable]] or difficult to [[discern]].
##: {{ux|en|the '''subtlety''' of the [[Mona Lisa]]’s smile}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1964|author={{w|Saul Bellow}}|title={{w|Herzog (novel)|Herzog}}|url=https://archive.org/details/herzog0000bell_s1p6|pages=248-249|publisher=Viking|location=New York
|passage={{...}} he had a lifetime of skill in interpreting his father’s gestures: those bent knees meant that something of great '''subtlety''' was about to be revealed.}}
## (''of things'') The quality of being done in a [[clever]] way that is not [[obvious]] or not [[direct]]; the quality of being [[carefully]] [[thought out]].
##: {{ux|en|the '''subtlety''' of a writer’s analysis / of a singer’s [[phrasing]]}}
##: {{syn|en|refinement}}
## (''of people'') The quality of being able to [[achieve]] one's aims through [[clever]], [[delicate]] or [[indirect]] methods.
##: ''With all his usual '''subtlety''', he quietly fixed the problem before anyone else noticed it.''
##: {{syn|en|discretion|finesse|savoir-faire}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1979|author={{w|William Styron}}|title={{w|Sophie's Choice (novel)|Sophie’s Choice}}|url=https://archive.org/details/sophieschoicesty00styr|chapter=3|page=74|publisher=Random House|location=New York
|passage=European women often boss their men too, but with a beguiling '''subtlety''' unknown to most American females.}}
## (''of people'') The quality of being able to [[notice]] or [[understand]] things that are not [[obvious]].
##: {{syn|en|acumen|perceptiveness|perspicacity}}
##* {{RQ:KJV|Proverbs|1|4|text=To give '''subtilty''' to the simple, to the young man knowledge and discretion.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1770|author={{w|Oliver Goldsmith}}|title=The Life of Henry St. John, Lord Viscount Bolingbroke|location=London|publisher=T. Davies|page=7|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004897239.0001.000
|passage=his '''subtilty''' in thinking and reasoning were profound,}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} An instance of being [[subtle]], a [[subtle]] thing, especially a subtle [[argument]] or [[distinction]].
#: {{ux|en|The '''subtleties''' of this overture are often overlooked.}}
#: {{syn|en|nicety|nuance}}
#* '''1561''', {{w|William Whittingham}} ''et al.'' (translators), ''{{w|Geneva Bible}},'' {{w|Book of Wisdom|Wisdom of Solomon}} 8.8,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A10675.0001.001]</sup>
#*: {{...}} she [Wisdom] knoweth the '''subtilties''' of wordes, and the solutions of darke sentences:
#* {{RQ:Boyle Sceptical Chymist|pages=14–15|pageref=14|passage=For I, and no doubt You, have long obſerved, that thoſe Dialectical '''ſubtleties''', that the Schoolmen too often employ about Phyſiological Myſteries, are vvont much more to declare the vvit of him that uſes them, then increaſe the knovvledge or remove the doubts of ſober lovers of truth. And such captious '''ſubtleties''' do indeed often puzzle and ſometimes ſilence men, but rarely ſatisfy them.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1779|author={{w|David Hume}}|title={{w|Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion}}|section=Part 10, p. 112|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004895521.0001.000
|passage=It is your turn now {{...}} to support your philosophical '''subtilties''' against the dictates of plain reason and experience.}}
#* {{RQ:Hardy Woodlanders|2|6|104|url=https://archive.org/details/woodlanders02hard/page/104|text=She could not explain the '''subtleties''' of her feeling as clearly as he could state his opinion, even though she had skill in speech, and her father had none.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1952|author={{w|John Steinbeck}}|title={{w|East of Eden (novel)|East of Eden}}|location=London|publisher=Heinemann|section=Part 4, Chapter 36, p. 366|url=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.458774/page/n371
|passage=His body was as insensitive to pain as was his mind to '''subtleties'''.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|historical}} An ornate medieval illusion dish or table decoration, especially when made from one thing but crafted to look like another.
#: {{ux|en|At the king's coronation feast, several '''subtleties''' were served between main courses.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1548|author={{w|Edward Hall}}|title=The Union of the Two Noble and Illustre Famelies of Lancastre [and] Yorke|location=London|publisher=Richard Grafton|chapter=The triumphaunt reigne of Kyng Henry the .VIII.|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A02595.0001.001
|passage=the seruice {{...}} was [[sumptuous|sumpteous]], with many '''subtleties''', [[strange|straunge]] [[device|deuises]], with seuerall poses, and many deintie dishes.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|countable|archaic}} The quality of being [[clever]] in [[surreptitious]] or [[deceitful]] [[behaviour]]; an act or argument that shows this quality.
#: {{syn|en|artifice|craftiness|cunning|deceitfulness|slyness|trickery}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1575|author={{w|George Gascoigne}}|title=The Noble Arte of Venerie of Hunting|location=London|publisher=Christopher Barker|chapter=Termes generall of the huntesman, in hunting of any chase|page=243|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A14021.0001.001
|passage=When eyther Hare or Deare, or any other [[chase]] [[use|vseth]] '''subtleties''' to [[deceive|deceyue]] the houndes, we saye they ''crosse'' or ''double''.}}
#* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|book=3|page=181|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A12231.0001.001|text=[She] resolued now with plainnesse to winne trust, which trust she might after [[deceive|deceyue]] with a greater '''subtletie'''.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|2 Corinthians|11|3|text=But I fear, lest by any means, as the serpent beguiled Eve through his '''subtilty''', so your minds should be corrupted from the simplicity that is in Christ.}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Iliad|volume=3, Observations on the Tenth Book|page=136|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004836009.0001.003|text={{...}} the Spy was deceiv’d rather by the Art and '''Subtlety''' of {{w|Odysseus|Ulysses}}, than by his Falshood.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Persuasion|4|10|page=220|url=https://archive.org/details/northangerabbeyp04aust/page/220|text=Mr. Elliot’s '''subtleties''', in endeavouring to prevent [the marriage]}}
# {{lb|en|countable|obsolete}} A [[trick]] that creates a [[false]] appearance.
#: {{syn|en|deception|illusion}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|V|1|text=You [[do|doe]] yet taste<br>Some '''subtleties''' o’ th’ Isle, that will not let you<br>[[believe|Beleeue]] things certaine:}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|obsolete}} The [[property]] of having a low [[density]] or thin [[consistency]].
#* '''1630''', {{w|Thomas Johnson (botanist)|Thomas Johnson}} (translator/editor), ''A Treatise of the Plague {{...}} Collected out of the workes of {{...}} {{w|Ambroise Paré|Ambrose Parey}},'' London, Chapter{{nbsp}}11, p.{{nbsp}}33,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A08913.0001.001]</sup>
#*: Therefore at Paris where naturally, and also through the aboundance of filth that is about the Citie, the Aire is darke and grosse, the pestilent Infection is lesse fierce and contagious then it is in Prouince, for the '''subtletie''' of the Aire stimulates or helps forward the Plague.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1692|author={{w|Robert Boyle}}|title=General Heads for the Natural History of a Country Great or Small Drawn Out for the Use of Travellers and Navigators|location=London|publisher=John Tailor and S. Hedford|page=3|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A28984.0001.001
|passage=About the Air is to be considered, its Temperature as to Heat, Dryness and Moisture, and the Measures of them, its Weight, Clearness, Refractive Power, its '''Subtilty''' or Grosness {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|obsolete}} The [[property]] of being able to [[penetrate]] [[material]]s easily.
#: {{syn|en|penetrancy|piercingness}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1760|author={{w|John Wesley}}|title={{w|The Desideratum; or, Electricity Made Plain and Useful}}|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004793050.0001.000|pages=37-38|location=London
|passage=Hence we see the amazing '''Subtlety''' of this Fire, which pervades Glass as readily as if nothing were in the Way.}}

